    "evidence_text": "Specificity Residues Determine Binding Affinity for Two-Component Signal Transduction Systems\n\nIn general, these reactions appear to be highly specific and require an appropriate association between the HK and RR proteins.\n\nThe data indicate that binding affinity is a critical parameter governing system-wide signaling fidelity for bacterial signal transduction proteins.   \n\nOur results demonstrate that previously identified specificity residues dictate binding affinity and that phosphatase specificity follows phosphotransfer specificity for cognate HK-RR pairs.\n\nThe data indicate that preferential binding affinity is the basis for signaling fidelity in bacterial two-component systems.\n\nInnate Immune and Chemically Triggered Oxidative Stress Modifies Translational Fidelity\n\nTranslational fidelity, essential for protein and cell function, requires accurate transfer RNA (tRNA) aminoacylation.\n\nThe accuracy of tRNA aminoacylation in vivo is uncertain, however, and might be considerably lower.\n\nAmong six amino acids tested, tRNA misacylation occurs exclusively with Met.\n\nDNA replication fidelity\n\nDNA replication fidelity is a key determinant of genome stability and is central to the evolution of species and to the origins of human diseases.\n\nUsing Structural Information to Change the Phosphotransfer Specificity of a Two-Component Chemotaxis Signalling Complex\n\nOne of the best understood two-component signalling pathways is the chemotaxis pathway.\n\nIn summary, our results provide valuable insight into how cells mediate specificity in one of the most abundant signalling pathways in biology, two-component signal transduction.\n\nDistinction and relationship between elongation rate and processivity of RNA polymerase II in vivo.\n\nA nano-positioning system for macromolecular structural analysis\n\nPolyphosphate (poly P) metabolism regulates the stress response in mycobacteria.\n\nThe ppk1 promoter of Mycobacterium tuberculosis is activated under phosphate starvation.\n\nBinding of phosphorylated RegX3 to this site was confirmed by electrophoretic mobility shift assay.\n\nIn vitro transcription corroborated the involvement of SigE in ppk1 transcription.\n\nThe biphasic behavior of incoherent feed-forward loops in biomolecular regulatory networks.\n\nWhy do the structurally identical incoherent FFLs play such different dynamical roles?\n\nAdditional Supporting Information may be found in the online version of the article.\n\nThus, TatC appears to be a specificity determinant for protein secretion via the Tat pathway.\n\nA mathematical investigation of the effects of inhibitor therapy on three putative phosphorylation cascades governing the two-component system of the agr operon.\n\nTwo-component systems (TCSs) are widely employed by bacteria to sense specific external signals and conduct an appropriate response via a phosphorylation cascade within the cell.",
